course: dictionary
lesson: 1614
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one is meant. Look at the words around the word, and at the subject you are learning. «Chemise» can mean a loose, old-fashioned dress or undergarment, or the French word for shirt.
explain: In a history lesson, a chemise is a piece of clothing that Victorian and Tudor women wore under their dresses. In a French lesson, «une chemise» is a shirt. The subject you are studying is a clue to the right meaning.
explain: A false friend is a word that looks like a word you know but means something different. Chemise is a false friend for English learners of French, so when you read French, do not use the old meaning. Swap your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
fact: Chemise is a noun: one chemise, two chemises.
fact: Meaning 1: a loose, simple dress, or a woman's undergarment like a long shirt, worn in the past.
fact: Meaning 2, in French: «chemise» means shirt.
fact: In the wolf's rhyme, «ma chemise, mon pantalon, mes chaussettes» means my shirt, my trousers, my socks.
fact: A false friend looks like a word you know but has a different meaning in another language.
exercise: «She wore a loose white chemise under her dress, as women did long ago.» What is a chemise here? | A modern pair of jeans | *A long-ago undergarment, like a long shirt | A kind of cake | A wooden chair
exercise: In a French lesson you read «ma chemise, mon pantalon, mes chaussettes». What does «ma chemise» mean? | my skirt | my dress | my hat | *my shirt
exercise: What is the French word for shirt? | pantalon | robe | *chemise | jupe
test: In a history lesson, «chemise» means what? | *a loose dress or undergarment worn in the past | a tall tree | a kind of bread | a French word for a bus
test: In a French lesson, «une chemise» means what? | a dress | *a shirt | a dressing gown | a skirt
exam: Which statement about chemise is correct? | It is a French word for a dress only | It means the same thing in every subject | *It has two meanings, and the subject you are studying tells you which one to use | It only ever means a skirt
